## Chapter 1.4: Spark into the Flame

Nadezha and Luka walked alongside each other, a mist coming out of their mouth every breath they exhaled. Luka started,

“So… what did you synced earlier?” Referring to the e-paper she rolled and tucked inside her winter coat deep pocket.

“Oh, this?” She pulled out the rolled e-paper and hand it to him, “take a look yourself,” she said as she handed it to him.

Luka unrolled the e-paper and read its content. The heading reads: Vehicle Dossier, with the content being a highly detailed information about a _Russo-Balt Burevestnik-GT/DVS_[footnote:fhj2d]. Upon swiping to the left, the full resolution image appeared: a beefy yellow sports car with an x-ray infographic of its internal combustion engine and specialized parts.

%Footnote.fhj2d: 

Luka was never a car guy, public transport or just simply traditional stroll was all he ever need. Though he did find the sleek design and the internal complexity of this car quite intriguing. He said as he handed it back to her, “Wow… Igor really didn’t hold back, huh?”

“Yup. That thing was definitely more expensive than the first floor on our station,” she laughed, “to be honest, I don’t mind someone with this kind of hobby but anything as expensive as this is always an orange flag.” She rolled the e-paper and put it back in her pocket as it belongs.

The way to the scrapyard was still a bit away, so Luka strike another, a little more casual question, “So, what’s your hobby, Officer Morozova? You said an orange flag; should I assume that your hobby is also quite expensive?” He grinned as he called out to what she said earlier.

“Not as expensive as this of course… But I have a side project,” she hesitated a bit but decided to share anyway, “a channel on Spark[footnote:f6m5z].” 

%Footnote.f6m5z: 

Luka eyes lit up. He didn’t expect an officer at law being an innovator and a content creator on Spark. “Oh! That’s great, actually, Alex is on it too. How many are your followers?” he asked eagerly, a genuine connection was being formed here.

“I started about a year ago, so just a couple thousands. I don’t upload that often anyway. I mostly support other’s project,” she replied humbly.

“Let’s exchange channel. I’ll send you Alex’s,” he offered and pulled out his phone, enter the Spark app and showed her Alex’s channel Touchy[footnote:f6fc1].

%Footnote.f6fc1: Touchy: a feature that allow an encrypted data transfer between device in a very close proximity (maximum 10 cm.). Mostly used to instantly share or exchange app-specific contact.

Nadezhda brought out her phone, opened her own channel and tap the phone lightly on Luka’s. Shortly, each of their channel displayed on their screen. 

“Elektrifinadtsiya…[footnote:f8ls9]” Luka tried to sound it out slowly, unfamiliar with its cadence until he realized. He chuckled, “Ah, clever wordplay! So you do electronic tinkering?” he asked while reading her channel tagline: “Elektrifinadtsiya, the concept of pushing normal electronics to its absolute limit just for the bit.”

%Footnote.f8ls9: Elektrifinadtsiya: a wordplay on Elektrifikatsiya which means electrification. Her channel name would roughly be localized as, “Electrifi-Nadya-tion.” Pronounce almost like “Electrifination,” with “na” sounded like “nya.”

“Uh huh. Not a new concept, to be frank, but it’s fun.” But once she glanced at her screen she was taken aback by the sheer follower count of Alex’s channel, “Woah! 100k!? ‘Ob Dynamo[footnote:fhmlh]…!’ I’ve seen his video recommended to me before! I never checked it out because it was too industrial for me but wow…” She scrolled down a to skim the channel then she noticed, “Wait, is this you?” 

%Footnote.fhmlh: Ob Dynamo: a name that Alex came up on a whim while looking out the window to see the Ob river in Novosibirsk where he used to study before being transferred to PIRM.

Luka glanced at her phone, “Yeah. I feature in his video sometimes. Usually, I’m his cameraman,” he replied. Though, the situation is kind of bittersweet at the moment. Since h e developed the vision, there was always this sense of awkwardness between him and Alex. Until this vision matter is resolved, their relationship would stay stagnant and lukewarm.

“I see. We should collab sometimes,” she gave a dry teasing laugh, half-serious, half-joke. 

They continued the get-to-know for a while before she said, “Now that you know this much about me, let’s drop the formality. Call me, Nadya.”

Luka’s friends were usually someone in his trade or academic circle. This was the first time that he ever had a friend who clearly wielded more authority. Despite being around the same age, the power gap still made it a bit awkward for him. Nonetheless, he had been allowed.

“I-I’m honored, Comr— sorry, Nadya,” he was a bit too excited.

“Look at you, making this all formal. Come on, can’t Militsiya girl have friend?,” she giggled, “So how should I call you, Lukasha or just Luka?” She asked.

“Just Luka is fine. My colleagues also call me that,” he replied.

“Luka it is then,” she reacher her hand out of her pocket for a handshake. 

“Nice to meet you, Luka.”  she offered a warm smile and warm hand.

“Me too,” Luka went for handshake.

After their wholesome interaction, they finally arrived at the scrapyard, the crime scene. There were already so many people, citizen and Militsiya alike, gathered around the perimeter. The red and white striped tape could be seen from a far. Multiple patrol cars and a single firetruck parked near it despite the fire had already been out.

Luka could sensed the excitement in Nadya as she every steps she took was faster and faster. He adjusted his pace to match her speed. Eventually they were close enough to see inside the scrapyard.

The first sight that caught their eyes was a dark burnt sports car, or what the remaining metal frame of it. It seemed that they arrived just in time to see the body retrieval process. Firefighters were carefully cutting the metal door to access the charred body inside.

“Excuse me, make way for officer please,” Nadya muttered as she scurried through the crowd with Luka trailing behind her. Once she reached the cordoning tape threshold, she turned back.

“Alright. Comrade Huo, this is where our ‘equality’ comes to an end…” She said sarcastically, knowing that only her can cross into the scene.

Luka catching what she implied, returned the humor, “Understood, Comrade Junior Lieutenant. I’ll be observing the situation with great interest.”

Nadya nodded. Ducking under the tape, she crossed into the perimeter, leaving Luka behind among the crowd.

Luka glanced around. Where he currently standing was the entrance, the rusty metal sign above him said, _Vtorchermet_[footnote:fdw5v]. Beyond that gate was an open yard with piles of scrap metals gathered close to the walls, primarily scrap from factories, train tracks, pressed car frames, and some parts he even recognized from PIRM. On the opposite wall opposite of the entrance housed an office building and a truck garage.

%Footnote.fdw5v: 

The star of the show, the sports car, was neatly positioned in the very middle of the yard away from other piles of metal. It’s unclear whether this was a way to intimidate the public or the perpetrator simply didn’t want to also be labeled an arsonist.

It seemed that by the time they arrived, the only thing that was left to be investigated is the body as others yellow tent of numbers already scattered around the area. The Militsiya officers gathered and watched from a safe distance, away from the firefighter excavators.

Luka saw Nadya talking with a male senior officer. He couldn’t hear what they were talking about because of the gossiping crowd and the loud metal cutting noise, but the senior one didn’t look very happy seeing her here.

After observing everything, he decided that he had seen enough. He didn’t have any interest steal the investigative duty from the officers. The moment he was about to pull out his phone, a finger tapped on his shoulder. He turned around.

“Little[footnote:f9vcz] Huo! Didn’t expect you here! Come to see the talk-of-the-town?” A woman stood in front of him, speaking to him in Dongbei[footnote:fdzq1] Chinese dialect. She wore round prescription glasses, a stylish winter coat with fur around the collar, on its left chest bore a printed symbol of Heilongjiang People’s Daily[footnote:fmzbs]. 

%Footnote.fdzq1: 

%Footnote.f9vcz: In Chinese, “Little” prefix means bla bla bla

%Footnote.fmzbs: 

“Ah, Sis[footnote:f7f8c], I don’t expect you here as well! Not like you’re not a reporter but because you haven’t cross my mind at all,” Luka sarcastically joked back in Dongbei. Other than Alex, Lin Chen was another close contact of him. They met the first time in a science symposium at PIRM about a month after Luka moved here. She was a representative reporter of People’s Daily in Proganichny for almost 6 years.

%Footnote.f7f8c: In Chinese, “Sis” prefix means bla bla bla

“Ouch… What did I do to be forgotten by Little Huo… Maybe I stop reporting your work next time~” she jested back, knowing he didn’t mean it.

“Speaking of which, I didn’t see you in Vladivostok last Sunday. Did you forget to come or…?”  Luka asked, calling back to the WSS jubilee that he featured as a guest speaker.

“Oh, no no, I didn’t forget. It’s just over there are outside of my coverage. Unlike you, I always keep you on my mind, I’ll let you know,” she raised an eyebrow, a slap back to his joke earlier, “Also, if I’m not wrong, there should be a middle-aged guy representing the People’s Daily there, yeah? He graduated from Tsinghua[footnote:fdktc] too, no joke with that one.”

%Footnote.fdktc: 

Looking back, there was certainly a serious-looking reporter that asked him many difficult questions, that must be him. Compare to Lin, his questions were more technical. when hers were more casual, more layman, so to speak.

“Oh yeah, that guy was tough. Felt like talking with Dr. Krotov. During his panel, they also exchanged a lot of… _discussion_,” he did an air-quote. He was glad that the heat wasn’t on him too when his panel came up, “But they seemed to get along quite well backstage though.”

“Heh, starting to miss me now after that guy?” She teasingly asked.

“Totally,” he let out admitted laugh and nodded.

“Anyway, how long were you here?” he asked.

“Since six. I’m just waiting for them to get the body out,” she replied but let out a small sigh, “This is the first time in my life I see a real corpse, but at the same time, this is probably my biggest scoop yet. I can’t just not do it…” 

She continued, “he was corrupt and all that but I don’t think anyone deserve this…” 

Her words let Luka to think about the implication of this too. Was Igor burn alive in this car? Being trapped in one’s beloved possession while being slowly engulfed by flame is a horrifying thought. Or perhaps, in a better scenario, maybe he was already dead or unconscious? Even so, having your corpse desecrated, burned to a crisp, with no recognizable identity left on the outside, is also too brutal. Whatever happened, the perpetrator must have really despised Igor.

“I agree, he should be brought to justice, not vigilantism,” Luka concurred.

Lin nodded in agreement. Then she lean to the side to see past Luka, “Ah, look, they already got the seat out,” Lin pointed out.

Luka turned back. The firefighter carefully carrying out the charred body that must have melted to the seat into the empty space. The crowd grasped, some in disgust, some in horror. People started gossiping with each other nervously.

“Alright, I have to go take a photo. Talk to you later, Little Huo!” she patted him on the back and scurried between the crowd to find the the angle.

He glanced around the yard again. The firefighter and the Millitsiya were discussing how to separate the body from the seat without damaging the body more than it already was. 

What’s on Luka mind though, was this morbid curiosity and a collection of indescribable feelings. Since he had developed the vision, he had only use that on Alex and Nadya. But… what about Igor? Of course, he was dead but his theoretical mind couldn’t stop nagging him.

“_Would seeing into his future, give me a clue about who did this?_” He thought. It was a wishful thinking, what could looking into the future tell about what happened in the past?

But all rationality had already been thrown outside the frontal lobe. Being a theorist is all about believing in a ridiculous notion. The innate curiosity of human took over him as he stared intently into the corpse and let his sight melted away…
